From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 00:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: mediatek: add BPI-R4 Pro system LED aliases

Linux upstream describes the red front-panel LED as the fault indicator
and the blue front-panel LED as the activity indicator. Wire those LEDs
to OpenWrt's standard system-state aliases so the generic diagnostic LED
handling can use them.

The LEDs are connected through a PCA9555 GPIO expander behind a PCA9548
I2C mux, so they register after preinit when those drivers are modular.
A board-specific boot service reapplies the regular preinit indication
after the normal kmodloader pass has completed.

Use the blue activity LED for boot and upgrade activity, and leave it off
after a healthy startup. Reserve the red fault LED for failsafe. The green
power LED remains controlled by the board power circuit.

Signed-off-by: Codex <codex@openai.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro.dtsi       | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro.dtsi
@@ -24,6 +24,9 @@
 		i2c4 = &imux1_sfp1;
 		i2c5 = &imux2_sfp2;
 		i2c6 = &imux3_wifi;
+		led-boot = &led_blue;
+		led-failsafe = &led_red;
+		led-upgrade = &led_blue;
 	};
 
 	chosen {
